## Conventions

- <Where durable knowledge goes vs. where sharable output goes.>
- Commit messages are plain and dated-by-git; **don't rewrite history** — the
  history IS the record.
- When updating a plan or a fact, **supersede rather than delete** — mark the old
  section superseded with a date and a pointer to what replaced it.
- Every brain file carries YAML frontmatter; run `python src/brain_lint.py`
  before committing brain changes (fix a stale index with `--write-index`).
  (Delete if there is no brain.)
- Tasks live in `plan/plan.json`, prioritized (P0/P1/P2) and split by owner
  (`me` / `agent`); regenerate `dashboard/index.html` with
  `python src/build_dashboard.py` after changing them. Keep it current — a stale
  plan is worse than none.
- Scoped staging only: `git add <paths>`, **never `git add -A`** — unrelated
  working-tree changes may belong to another session.
- Commit freely; **never push without the owner's go-ahead** (enforced in
  `.claude/settings.json`).
- Project skills: <list the `/slash` skills this repo defines and one line each.>

## Interaction

**Ask with buttons, not prose.** Whenever you put a choice to the owner — at any
point in the work, not only at the end — present the options as a clickable
menu via the `AskUserQuestion` tool rather than writing them out as free text.
Put the recommended option first, marked "(Recommended)". Reserve free-text
questions for genuinely open-ended asks that have no discrete options.

**End a unit of work with a next-step menu.** When you finish a unit of work, end
the response with a 2–4 option `AskUserQuestion` menu of concrete next steps.
Skip the menu only when you're already asking a question, when you're mid-task,
or when there's a single obvious next action — then just proceed and mention it.

